<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>Atlas.Api.IntegrationTests</RootNamespace>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Del 16, second half. Atlas.Application.Tests (the unit-test project sitting
    next to this one) mocks every repository/service dependency and never
    touches a real database, Azurite, Redis, or Service Bus — it proves
    TicketService/AuthService/etc. orchestrate correctly in isolation. This
    project proves the opposite thing: that a real HTTP request, through real
    JWT authentication, real [Authorize(Policy=...)] enforcement, real routing,
    and a real LocalDB database (via WebApplicationFactory<Program> — see
    Program.cs's `public partial class Program {}` marker, added ahead of time
    during Del 14 for exactly this), actually produces the right status code
    and JSON shape. Two things can ONLY be tested here, not in
    Atlas.Application.Tests: the self-role-change/self-deactivate guard in
    UsersController (it reads ICurrentUserService, which only means something
    with a real authenticated HttpContext behind it), and EF Core's navigation-
    property fixup (e.g. TicketTag.Tag resolving to a real tag name) — see the
    doc comment on TicketServiceTests.AddTagAsync_... in Atlas.Application.Tests
    for the full reasoning on why a mocked unit test can't observe that.

    A direct ProjectReference to Atlas.Api itself (not just Atlas.Application)
    is required for WebApplicationFactory<Program> to resolve ContentRootPath
    correctly out of the box — Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing's MSBuild
    targets emit a [assembly: WebApplicationFactoryContentRootAttribute]
    pointing at Atlas.Api's own output only when there's a direct reference to
    it, which sidesteps the manual - ger ett errror om det är ihop-contentRoot / pwd -W debugging saga this
    project already went through once for a *different* problem during Del 14
    (see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) — no reason to invite a repeat of that here.

    Prerequisites to actually run this project locally (all already required
    by `dotnet run`ning Atlas.Api itself in Development, per README.md "Kom
    igång" — nothing new except the dedicated AtlasDb_Test database, which
    ApiFactory creates and migrates itself):
      - LocalDB (AtlasDb_Test — separate from the AtlasDb a `dotnet run`
        session might have open in SSMS/Azure Data Studio at the same time)
      - Azurite running (BlobStorage:ConnectionString = UseDevelopmentStorage=true)
      - A local Redis container (`docker run -p 6379:6379 redis`)
      - `az login` (Service Bus's DefaultAzureCredential chain — see
        DependencyInjection.AddMessaging's doc comment; ExcludeManagedIdentityCredential
        is already set to true in appsettings.Testing.json, same as Development)
    These are NOT started automatically by `dotnet test` — see the "Category=Integration"
    trait every test class in this project carries, and azure-pipelines.yml's
    `- -filter "Category!=Integration"`, for why CI's ubuntu-latest agent (which
    has none of the above) never attempts to run them.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Atlas.Api\Atlas.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.12.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
